  "introductionText": "What FRUS answers directly: the printed record shows corruption entering U.S. decisions in recurring ways: as a pretext a debtor government used against arbitral awards (Venezuela, 1875); as a diagnosis that shaped how far Washington would back a client (Nationalist China, 1947–48; South Korea, 1961); as an obstacle to a war aim that the United States chose not to override at the cost of sovereignty (South Vietnam, 1966–67); and, from 1975, as American corporate bribery abroad that became a matter of diplomacy (Lockheed and Japan, West Germany) and of trade policy (the Foreign Corrupt Practices Act). About 2,700 documents in about 435 of the 553 volumes use the words corruption or bribery, and the rate rises sharply after 1945. Where FRUS points for the rest: the documents came chiefly from the State Department's Central Decimal File (RG 59), from the National Security Files and President's Office Files of the Johnson and Kennedy Libraries, from the Ford Library's Presidential Correspondence with Foreign Leaders, and from Executive Secretariat lot files such as Lot 63D351 (RG 59, Records Relating to National Security Council Policy Papers, Entry A1 1586E, NAID 2839192, 1947–1979, partly restricted); the editors cite without printing further decimal files on South Korean and Indochinese internal politics and Lot 66D95 (same series) and Lot 62D1 (RG 59, Policy Planning Council, NAID 2838992). What this request cannot get from FRUS: the series ends in the late 1980s for this subject (about 6,000 dated documents in the 1980s against 76,000 in the 1940s), so the later anti-corruption regime is outside it, and the modern word 'kleptocracy' never appears (controls passed), which shows only that contemporaries said 'corruption'; 'questionable payments', the 1975–76 term for corporate bribery, cannot be counted because the index folds it into unrelated phrases; enforcement of the 1977 anti-bribery law appears in four documents in two volumes, so the Justice Department's and the SEC's own files are the shelf for that; and the 1867–68 Venezuelan commission scandal itself is printed only as Venezuela's charge, not as an inquiry.",
